Since your EKS cluster already exists, let’s find the EKS node security group and then allow it in db_sg.

That way your backend pods can connect to PostgreSQL.

Step 1 — Find EKS node security group in AWS Console

Go to:

EC2 instances

Click any worker EC2 instance.

Then under Security you'll see SG like:

eks-cluster-sg-xxxx
eks-node-group-sg-yyyy

Copy the one attached to worker nodes.

Example:

sg-0123456789abcdef
Faster way (EC2 console)

Go:

EC2 → Instances

Search:

EKS

Open worker instance.

Under Security tab:

Copy SG:

eks-node-group-sg

Step 2 — Where to use it

In your Terraform:

resource "aws_security_group" "db_sg" {
  name   = "db-sg"
  vpc_id = aws_vpc.main.id

  ingress {
    from_port = 5432
    to_port   = 5432
    protocol  = "tcp"

    security_groups = [
      aws_security_group.app_sg.id,
      "sg-0123456789abcdef"
    ]
  }

  egress {
    from_port   = 0
    to_port     = 0
    protocol    = "-1"
    cidr_blocks = ["0.0.0.0/0"]
  }
}

terraform validate
terraform plan
terraform apply

Why this matters

Traffic path:

Backend pod
   ↓
EKS worker node SG
   ↓
RDS db_sg

If DB only allows app_sg:

❌ pod cannot connect

If DB allows node SG:

✅ PostgreSQL works

Add this below your DB security group section:

####################################
# RDS Subnet Group
####################################
resource "aws_db_subnet_group" "db_subnet_group" {
  name = "app-db-subnet-group"

  subnet_ids = [
    aws_subnet.private_1.id,
    aws_subnet.private_2.id
  ]

  tags = {
    Name = "app-db-subnet-group"
  }
}

####################################
# PostgreSQL RDS Instance
####################################
resource "aws_db_instance" "postgres" {
  identifier = "app-postgres-db"

  engine         = "postgres"
  engine_version = "15.5"

  instance_class = "db.t3.micro"

  allocated_storage = 20
  storage_type      = "gp2"

  db_name  = "appdb"
  username = "postgres"
  password = "YourStrongPassword123!"

  db_subnet_group_name   = aws_db_subnet_group.db_subnet_group.name
  vpc_security_group_ids = [aws_security_group.db_sg.id]

  publicly_accessible = false

  skip_final_snapshot = true

  tags = {
    Name = "app-postgres-db"
  }
}

Then run:
terraform fmt
terraform validate
terraform plan

Expected:

Plan: 2 to add, 0 to change, 0 to destroy

Then:

terraform apply

Then we’ll do the next step:
connect backend/EKS app to PostgreSQL using the RDS endpoint.
